  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM EST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\n- Light rain possible today/tonight\n\nWater vapor imagery shows a short wave in western IL being nudged\neastward by another short wave dropping south across northwest IA within\na deepening upper trough. The IL short wave is producing light \nrain across the northern half of the cwa, with much more sparsely \npopulated echos south of I-96. A wider radar view shows more \nshowers/storms moving across northern IL. Model trends point \ntoward the IL thunder remaining south of the cwa. All of the \nprecipitation tonight will be light rain. \n\n- Cooler Thursday, lake shore rain/snow showers through Saturday\n\nThe IA short wave will drag down colder air tomorrow as it pivots\nthrough the upper trough. H8 temperatures near +2c today will fall\nto -7c Thursday. The north flow that will be generated by the\nshort wave will coupled with the cold advection will produce lake\neffect rain showers mostly over the lake, but clipping the lake\nshore too. While boundary layer air will be too warm for snow\nThursday, that could change Thursday night as the temperature\ndrops. If any snow does develop Thursday night, the lake shore\nnorth of Whitehall has the highest chance of seeing it. That area\ncould potentially see a few tenths of an inch of snow. The bulk of\nthe precipitation will be just west of the lake shore falling over\nthe lake. \n\nThe upper trough will remain over the state through Saturday\nbefore beginning to lift northeastward. The colder air will remain\ntoo with h8 temps in the -6c to -8c range through the weekend.\nThat will produce surface temperatures in the mid 40s for highs \nthrough Monday before the trough moves east and then ridging noses\ninto the region. At that point highs will climb into the 50s \nearly/mid week.",
